WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
If you have been writing tests in Xcode 16 using modern Swift testing macros,

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
you already possess the exact mental model required for JavaScript testing.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
At first glance, moving from the compiled environment of Swift

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
to the interpreted world of JavaScript feels like a disconnect in how logic is processed.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
But beneath the surface tooling, the structural logic remains the same.

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.000
You isolate a piece of behavior, assert that it produces a specific result, and repeat.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.000
We are going to map your existing iOS knowledge directly onto this new environment

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
using a translation matrix.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
We will start by resolving this simplest friction point,

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
the superficial vocabulary differences, and how tests are structured and grouped.

11
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Then we will tackle the core architectural difference,

12
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.000
moving from Swift's compile time dependency injection to just runtime module interception.

13
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Finally, we will expose the invisible engine divide between the node JS test environment

14
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
and the actual Hermes mobile runtime.

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Your existing iOS testing intuition is your greatest asset.

16
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Once you map it to this matrix, you will be able to contribute to a react native test suite

17
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
with the same rigor you use in Xcode.

18
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Both ecosystems require you to organize isolated behaviors into logical groups,

19
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
abandoning classes on the JavaScript side.

20
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The Swift at Suite annotation maps directly to Jests described block.

21
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Inside, the Swift at test function is identical to Jests it or test function.

22
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
In Swift, state isolation is handled automatically.

23
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Because at Suite structs get a fresh instance per test, placing your setup logic in the init method guarantees a clean slate.

24
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Jests does not have this struct lifecycle.

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
To achieve that same state isolation, you must pass explicit setup logic into the before each hook.

26
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
The tear down process follows the same logic.

27
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
A Swift at Suite class DNit method maps directly to Jests after each hook.

28
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
And just like nesting at suite types for specific sub scenarios, you can nest describe blocks and Jests.

29
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.000
The setup and tear down hooks will respect these nested scopes running from the outside in.

30
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:17.000
So while just lack Swift's object-oriented containers, it sculpt hooks provide identical lifecycle control and state isolation.

31
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Swift testing replaces the old access cert family with macros.

32
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Just centralizes everything into a single expect function, which is then chained to specific matcher methods.

33
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Equality checking is where precision matters.

34
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.000
To mirror a primitive comparison in Swift, use .2b. This enforces strict reference equality.

35
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
But if comparing arrays or complex objects, .2b will fail.

36
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.000
You must use .2 for a deep structural comparison of the nested data.

37
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:55.000
When an API returns a massive payload and you only care about three specific fields, use .2b match object for a partial match.

38
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Now a critical warning. Just has no equivalent to Swift's hash-require macro.

39
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.000
There are no hard stops. If an assertion fails, just does not halt the test.

40
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Execution will continue through the rest of the block.

41
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Moving to asynchronous execution, the mapping is seamless.

42
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Swift's native concurrency model shares the same architecture as modern JavaScript promises.

43
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:30.000
A at-test async-throws signature translates perfectly to appending the async keyword to the just test function and awaiting the async calls inside.

44
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
If you encounter legacy just code utilizing the done callback pattern, avoid it entirely.

45
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Do not mix it with modern syntax.

46
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
The done callback is the JavaScript equivalent of the outdated X-test expectation.

47
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Modern just relies entirely on async and await.

48
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Here is the most significant architectural divergence between the two ecosystems, how they handle dependencies.

49
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Swift achieves testability at compile time. You define a protocol, implement a lightweight struct to act as a mock, and inject it into the type you are testing.

50
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Just operates at runtime. Instead of injecting dependencies, it intercepts the module system using just.mock.

51
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:22.000
This relies on hoisting. Just physically relocates your mock to the top of the evaluation chain, replacing the module before any other code is read.

52
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.000
If replacing the entire file is too destructive, you can use just.spyon.

53
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.000
This is a precision tool that wraps a single specific method while leaving the rest of the module intact.

54
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.000
To evaluate the intercepted calls, you use just.fn to create a standalone mock function.

55
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.000
This acts exactly like a lightweight Swift mock struct.

56
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Every time the mock is invoked, it records the artimates and stores them inside its.mock.calls property for you to assert against.

57
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:58.000
To succeed in just, you must stop trying to inject dependencies through protocols and learn to intercept imports at the module level.

58
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:05.000
When evaluating UI logic, React imposes a strict technical constraint. Custom hooks cannot be evaluated in a vacuum.

59
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.000
They require a React runtime context to exist.

60
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:17.000
To handle this, at testing library slash React Native package provides render hook, the designated tool to execute and track state updates.

61
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:24.000
The single most common cause of false test failures in React Native is forgetting to flush react effects before your assertions run.

62
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Every single action that triggers a state update must be wrapped inside an act function.

63
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
This forces React to process the queue before just evaluates the result.

64
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:42.000
For visual output validation, just utilizes snapshot testing. This maps directly to iOS UI test baseline images.

65
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:49.000
The component renders, serializes the output to a text file, and fails the test if that text file changes on the next run.

66
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.000
But snapshots carry a severe risk of snapshot blindness.

67
00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:05.000
If a component receives dynamic data, like timestamps or API responses, the snapshot will break constantly, generating low information failures that developers quickly learn to ignore.

68
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Restrict snapshot tests exclusively to leaf components with pure, logicalist presentation. This protects your test suite from accidental regressions without generating daily false positives.

69
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:23.000
There is one final complexity to testing in React Native, and it concerns the execution engine itself.

70
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:29.000
Production React Native executes JavaScript via the Hermes engine, but chest doesn't run inside Hermes.

71
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:36.000
It executes your tests strictly inside a Node.js V8 environment. This split creates practical dangers.

72
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Hermes has specific runtime behaviors for dates and regular expressions that V8 doesn't share.

73
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Because of this engine disparity, any performance benchmarks written as chest tests are invalid for predicting production speeds.

74
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.000
Furthermore, Hermes deliberately disables certain JavaScript introspection features for security.

75
00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Code relying on those features will pass in your chest test suite, and then crash on a physical device.

76
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:11.000
We have mapped the architecture, the assertions, the async life cycles, and the mocking patterns. The matrix is complete.

77
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Just provides absolute confidence in your architectural logic and behavior, but remember the engine divide.

78
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.000
True operational fidelity still requires manual verification on a physical device.

